WEBVTT

01:42.270 --> 01:44.021
You work too hard.

01:45.606 --> 01:47.149
Where is everyone?

01:47.275 --> 01:48.693
They refused to come in.

01:50.069 --> 01:51.237
Why?

01:51.529 --> 01:53.322
It's Saturday.

01:55.741 --> 01:56.909
Why did you come in?

01:56.993 --> 01:58.077
I work too hard.

01:58.494 --> 01:59.787
It's boiling in here.

02:14.677 --> 02:17.138
Eight milliroentgen. A leak?

02:17.221 --> 02:18.222
No.

02:19.682 --> 02:22.018
It would've gone off before.
It's coming from outside.

02:22.768 --> 02:23.853
The Americans?

03:04.602 --> 03:05.895
Iodine-131.

03:07.063 --> 03:10.316
It's not military.
It's uranium decay, U-235.

03:11.067 --> 03:12.068
Reactor fuel?

03:15.196 --> 03:17.948
Ignalina. Maybe, uh,
240 kilometers away.

03:27.792 --> 03:30.753
Yes, this is Ulana Khomyuk
with the Institute of Nuclear...

03:32.171 --> 03:33.172
Looking for...

03:38.427 --> 03:40.763
- All right, stay calm.
- Don't tell me to stay calm.

03:44.600 --> 03:47.103
They're at four. It's not them.

03:47.770 --> 03:48.771
Who's the next closest?

03:49.313 --> 03:51.899
It's Chernobyl, but that's not
possible. They're 400 kilometers away.

03:52.400 --> 03:54.318
That's too far
for eight milliroentgen.

03:54.402 --> 03:55.611
They'd have to be split open.

03:56.404 --> 03:57.655
Maybe they know something.

04:06.122 --> 04:07.957
- Iodine.
- Could it be a waste dump?

04:08.707 --> 04:09.708
No.

04:10.000 --> 04:11.585
We'd be seeing other isotopes.

04:12.378 --> 04:13.963
Nuclear test? Uh...

04:14.839 --> 04:15.881
New kind of bomb?

04:15.965 --> 04:17.883
We'd have heard. That's what
half our people work on here.

04:20.553 --> 04:24.640
Something with the space program
like a satellite or...

04:27.184 --> 04:28.352
No one's answering the phone.

04:49.665 --> 04:51.083
Get everyone started on an IV.

04:51.167 --> 04:52.168
We don't have enough.

04:52.251 --> 04:54.086
- All the children, then.
- But we don't have enough!

04:54.170 --> 04:55.212
As many as you can!

04:55.838 --> 04:58.340
- Where's the old man?
- He's set up a burn ward in 16.

05:13.314 --> 05:14.523
What are you doing? What is that?

05:14.690 --> 05:17.026
Milk. It's milk.
Much better than water.

05:17.109 --> 05:18.486
No, no, no. Stop. Stop!

05:18.569 --> 05:19.570
What are you doing?

05:19.653 --> 05:20.905
These are radiation burns!

05:20.988 --> 05:22.615
Their clothes are contaminated!

05:23.157 --> 05:24.658
Help me!

05:26.118 --> 05:27.119
Get it all off.

05:28.787 --> 05:30.498
We're taking it down to the basement.

05:33.667 --> 05:35.127
He was a firefighter from Chernobyl.

05:35.211 --> 05:36.629
Can you stop pushing, please?

05:40.925 --> 05:42.009
We have our orders!

05:42.510 --> 05:44.470
- Disperse!
- Hey, there! Get him!

05:47.056 --> 05:48.641
Why are you
keeping us out here?

06:45.531 --> 06:46.532
Professor Legasov?

06:47.491 --> 06:48.534
Oh, no, not yet.

06:49.118 --> 06:51.453
They're finishing up some other
business. It'll be a few more minutes.

06:51.537 --> 06:52.580
Can I get you some tea?

06:52.663 --> 06:54.039
No, I'm fine. Thank you.

06:54.123 --> 06:55.958
Would you care to read
Deputy Chairman Shcherbina's report

06:56.041 --> 06:57.042
while you're waiting?

06:57.126 --> 06:58.294
Certainly. Thank you.

07:58.437 --> 08:00.022
All right, Professor.
They're ready.

08:01.815 --> 08:03.442
Professor Legasov?

08:28.926 --> 08:30.094
You have my support.

08:37.393 --> 08:39.311
Thank you all
for your duty to this commission.

08:39.395 --> 08:42.398
We will begin with
Deputy Chairman Shcherbina's briefing,

08:42.481 --> 08:45.943
and then, we will discuss
next steps if necessary.

08:46.235 --> 08:48.112
Thank you,
comrade General Secretary.

08:48.195 --> 08:52.533
I'm pleased to report that
the situation in Chernobyl is stable.

08:52.616 --> 08:55.411
Military and civilian patrols
have secured the region,

08:55.494 --> 08:57.371
and Colonel General Pikalov,

08:58.080 --> 09:00.791
who commands troops specializing
in chemical hazards,

09:00.874 --> 09:02.710
has been dispatched to the plant.

09:03.043 --> 09:04.378
In terms of radiation,

09:04.461 --> 09:08.674
plant director Bryukhanov
reports no more than 3.6 roentgen.

09:09.091 --> 09:11.635
I'm told it's the equivalent
of a chest X-ray.

09:11.719 --> 09:14.263
So if you're overdue for a check-up...

09:14.638 --> 09:16.724
- And foreign press?
- Totally unaware.

09:17.433 --> 09:19.810
KGB First Deputy Chairman
Charkov assures me

09:19.893 --> 09:22.563
that we have successfully
protected our security interests.

09:23.147 --> 09:25.232
Good. Very good.

09:25.649 --> 09:27.985
Well, it seems like
it's well in hand, so...

09:29.445 --> 09:31.905
If there's nothing else,
meeting adjourned.

09:33.198 --> 09:34.199
No!

09:37.161 --> 09:38.162
Pardon me?

09:39.204 --> 09:40.873
Uh, we can't adjourn.

09:41.498 --> 09:43.834
This is Professor Legasov
of the Kurchatov Institute.

09:44.251 --> 09:45.836
Professor, if you have any concerns,

09:45.919 --> 09:47.713
feel free to address them
with me later.

09:47.796 --> 09:50.716
I can't. I am sorry. I'm so sorry.

09:51.675 --> 09:55.304
Page three, the section on casualties.
Uh...

09:56.263 --> 09:59.183
"A fireman was severely
burned on his hand

09:59.266 --> 10:01.602
"by a chunk of smooth, black mineral

10:01.685 --> 10:04.188
"on the ground,
outside the reactor building."

10:04.271 --> 10:06.106
Smooth, black mineral, graphite.

10:06.190 --> 10:08.025
There's graphite on the ground.

10:10.986 --> 10:13.989
Well, there was a tank explosion.
There's debris.

10:14.364 --> 10:15.532
Of what importance that could be...

10:15.616 --> 10:19.203
There's only one place in the entire
facility where you will find graphite.

10:19.286 --> 10:20.370
Inside the core.

10:20.454 --> 10:22.122
If there's graphite
on the ground outside,

10:22.206 --> 10:24.374
it means it wasn't
a control system tank that exploded.

10:24.458 --> 10:26.752
It was the reactor core. It's open!

10:30.047 --> 10:31.048
Um...

10:31.757 --> 10:32.841
Comrade Shcherbina?

10:32.925 --> 10:34.885
Comrade General Secretary,
I can assure you

10:34.968 --> 10:36.470
that Professor Legasov is mistaken.

10:37.054 --> 10:39.389
Bryukhanov reports that
the reactor core is intact.

10:39.556 --> 10:41.975
- And as for the radiation...
- Yes, 3.6 roentgen,

10:42.059 --> 10:44.520
which, by the way, is not
the equivalent of one chest X-ray,

10:44.603 --> 10:46.897
but rather 400 chest X-rays.

10:46.980 --> 10:49.316
That number's been bothering me
for a different reason, though.

10:49.817 --> 10:53.153
It's also the maximum reading
on low-limit dosimeters.

10:53.237 --> 10:55.322
They gave us the number they had.

10:56.156 --> 10:58.951
I think the true number
is much, much higher.

10:59.034 --> 11:01.620
If I'm right, this fireman
was holding the equivalent

11:01.703 --> 11:04.164
of four million chest X-rays
in his hand.

11:04.248 --> 11:06.542
Professor Legasov, there's no place
for alarmist hysteria...

11:06.625 --> 11:08.085
It's not alarmist if it's a fact!

11:08.168 --> 11:10.546
Well, I don't hear any facts at all.

11:11.755 --> 11:14.383
All I hear is a man I don't know

11:14.466 --> 11:17.553
engaging in conjecture
in direct contradiction

11:17.636 --> 11:19.638
to what has been reported
by party officials.

11:19.972 --> 11:22.099
I'm, uh... I apologize.

11:23.183 --> 11:25.143
I didn't mean, uh...

11:25.811 --> 11:29.398
Please, may I express my concern

11:29.481 --> 11:32.901
as calmly and as respectfully as I...

11:32.985 --> 11:35.696
- Professor Legasov...
- Boris.

11:36.655 --> 11:37.698
I will allow it.

11:47.416 --> 11:48.750
Um...

11:50.878 --> 11:52.629
An RBMK reactor

11:53.630 --> 11:57.259
uses uranium-235 as fuel.

11:58.427 --> 12:02.389
Every atom of U-235 is like a bullet

12:03.056 --> 12:05.183
traveling at nearly
the speed of light,

12:05.434 --> 12:07.603
penetrating everything in its path,

12:07.686 --> 12:09.771
woods, metal, concrete, flesh.

12:11.189 --> 12:14.151
Every gram of U-235 holds

12:14.234 --> 12:17.195
over a billion trillion
of these bullets.

12:17.279 --> 12:18.822
That's in one gram.

12:18.906 --> 12:21.909
Now, Chernobyl holds
over three million grams,

12:21.992 --> 12:23.452
and right now, it is on fire.

12:24.912 --> 12:29.041
Winds will carry radioactive particles
across the entire continent,

12:29.499 --> 12:31.084
rain will bring them down on us.

12:31.168 --> 12:34.296
That's three million
billion trillion bullets

12:34.379 --> 12:37.841
in the...
In the air we breathe,

12:38.258 --> 12:40.761
the water we drink, the food we eat.

12:43.347 --> 12:46.725
Most of these bullets
will not stop firing for 100 years.

12:47.809 --> 12:50.812
Some of them, not for 50,000 years.

12:51.104 --> 12:53.815
Yes, and this concern

12:54.942 --> 12:58.445
stems entirely from
the description of a rock?

13:03.158 --> 13:04.159
Yes.

13:06.828 --> 13:07.829
Hmm.

13:11.375 --> 13:12.376
Comrade Shcherbina...

13:14.044 --> 13:15.754
I want you to go to Chernobyl.

13:16.630 --> 13:18.590
You take a look at the reactor,
you personally,

13:18.840 --> 13:20.717
and you report directly back to me.

13:20.926 --> 13:23.178
A wise decision,
comrade General Secretary...

13:23.261 --> 13:24.888
And take Professor Legasov with you.

13:26.598 --> 13:27.891
Uh...

13:28.225 --> 13:31.144
Forgive me, comrade
General Secretary, but I...

13:31.228 --> 13:33.188
Do you know how
a nuclear reactor works?

13:35.649 --> 13:36.984
- No.
- No.

13:37.067 --> 13:39.027
Well, then how will you know
what you're looking at?

13:40.404 --> 13:41.571
Meeting adjourned.

14:41.381 --> 14:42.966
How does a nuclear reactor work?

14:43.050 --> 14:45.302
- What?
- It's a simple question.

14:47.304 --> 14:49.181
It's hardly a simple answer.

14:49.264 --> 14:52.517
Of course, you presume
I'm too stupid to understand.

14:53.351 --> 14:56.646
So I'll restate.
Tell me how a nuclear reactor works,

14:56.730 --> 14:59.274
or I'll have one of these soldiers
throw you out of the helicopter.

15:09.785 --> 15:13.121
A nuclear reactor makes
electricity with steam.

15:13.663 --> 15:16.583
The steam turns a turbine
which generates electricity.

15:17.000 --> 15:18.543
Where a typical power plant

15:19.086 --> 15:20.879
makes steam by burning coal,

15:21.254 --> 15:22.923
a nuclear plant...

15:28.470 --> 15:32.516
In a nuclear plant,
we use something called fission.

15:34.142 --> 15:38.814
We take an unstable element
like uranium-235,

15:39.564 --> 15:41.566
which has too many neutrons.

15:41.650 --> 15:43.568
- A neutron is, uh...
- The bullet.

15:45.946 --> 15:47.614
Yes, the bullet.

15:48.740 --> 15:51.576
So, bullets are flying off
of uranium.

15:51.660 --> 15:52.661
Now,

15:52.744 --> 15:57.374
if we put enough uranium atoms
close together,

15:58.250 --> 16:01.670
the bullets from one atom
will eventually strike another atom.

16:02.546 --> 16:05.590
The force of this impact
splits that atom apart,

16:05.674 --> 16:08.760
releasing a tremendous
amount of energy, fission.

16:09.136 --> 16:11.763
- And the graphite?
- Ah... Yes.

16:12.097 --> 16:15.934
The neutrons are actually traveling
so fast, we call this "flux."

16:16.309 --> 16:18.812
It's relatively unlikely
that the uranium atoms

16:18.937 --> 16:20.188
will ever hit one another.

16:21.022 --> 16:25.694
In RBMK reactors, we surround
the fuel rods with graphite

16:25.777 --> 16:29.948
to moderate, slow down,
the neutron flux.

16:33.994 --> 16:35.036
Good.

16:35.996 --> 16:38.290
I know how a nuclear reactor works.

16:40.500 --> 16:41.918
Now I don't need you.

17:01.771 --> 17:03.607
Can you help me?
I need to find my husband.

17:03.690 --> 17:04.733
No. Not now.

17:09.196 --> 17:10.405
- Mikhail!
- Lyudmilla.

17:10.488 --> 17:12.115
Take her.

17:12.449 --> 17:14.201
Take her away from here, please.

17:14.284 --> 17:16.953
Get away from them!
You want to get sick? Go!

17:17.120 --> 17:18.413
Please take her, please take her.

17:18.496 --> 17:19.831
Please take her, please.

17:19.915 --> 17:22.042
Please! Please!

17:22.375 --> 17:23.960
Please take her!

17:28.048 --> 17:29.174
Um...

17:29.674 --> 17:32.260
Excuse me,
I am Vasily Ignatenko's wife.

17:32.594 --> 17:34.846
He's a fireman. Ignatenko.

17:36.598 --> 17:39.267
Ignatenko, Sixth Paramilitary
Fire and Rescue Unit.

17:39.351 --> 17:41.645
I need to find him. Please.

17:45.565 --> 17:47.025
Ignatenko...

17:47.108 --> 17:49.319
He's being transported
by helicopter to Moscow.

17:49.819 --> 17:50.946
Hospital number six.

17:51.488 --> 17:53.573
Why, is he all right? Can I see him?

17:53.657 --> 17:55.033
You want to see him? Go to Moscow.

17:55.492 --> 17:57.160
But they're not letting us leave here.

18:00.163 --> 18:01.623
Tell them Major Burov allows it.

18:03.875 --> 18:05.377
When are they taking him?

18:06.253 --> 18:07.254
Now.

18:38.159 --> 18:39.577
We're approaching the power plant.

18:45.959 --> 18:47.168
What have they done?

18:48.962 --> 18:50.130
Can you see inside?

18:50.547 --> 18:51.756
I don't have to.

18:52.924 --> 18:55.760
Look. That's graphite on the roof.

18:55.844 --> 18:58.013
The whole building's been blown open.

18:58.096 --> 18:59.472
The core's exposed!

18:59.556 --> 19:01.433
I can't see how you can
tell that from here.

19:01.516 --> 19:03.893
For God's sakes. Look at that glow!

19:04.519 --> 19:07.063
That's radiation ionizing the air!

19:07.272 --> 19:08.982
Well, if we can't see, we don't know.

19:09.232 --> 19:10.483
Get us directly over the building!

19:10.567 --> 19:11.985
- Boris...
- Don't use my name!

19:12.068 --> 19:14.612
If we fly directly over an open
reactor, we'll be dead within a week!

19:14.696 --> 19:15.739
Dead!

19:17.240 --> 19:18.325
Sir?

19:18.408 --> 19:21.244
Get us over that building,
or I'll have you shot!

19:25.749 --> 19:27.667
If you fly directly over that core,
I promise you,

19:27.751 --> 19:29.461
by tomorrow morning,
you'll be begging for that bullet.

20:18.134 --> 20:19.844
Perhaps if you came back another day.

20:25.141 --> 20:26.643
Just wonderful.

20:27.727 --> 20:29.312
Deputy Secretary Garanin.

20:30.522 --> 20:34.234
Ulana Khomyuk of the Byelorussian
Institute for Nuclear Energy.

20:34.401 --> 20:35.402
What a pleasure.

20:35.819 --> 20:37.946
- Let me introduce...
- I'm here about Chernobyl.

20:40.698 --> 20:41.908
Such a lovely time.

20:42.575 --> 20:44.828
- Visit again, soon.
- I will. Thank you.

20:57.757 --> 21:00.510
I must tell you,
this is why no one likes scientists.

21:01.302 --> 21:03.388
When we have a disease to cure,
where are they?

21:03.721 --> 21:07.100
In a lab, noses in their books,
and so Grandma dies.

21:07.725 --> 21:10.228
But when there isn't a problem,
they're everywhere, spreading fear.

21:10.311 --> 21:11.729
I know about Chernobyl.

21:11.813 --> 21:12.981
Oh?

21:13.064 --> 21:15.900
I know that the core is either
partially or completely exposed.

21:15.984 --> 21:18.319
- Whatever that means.
- And that if you don't immediately

21:18.403 --> 21:21.114
issue iodine tablets
and then evacuate this city,

21:21.197 --> 21:23.450
hundreds of thousands of people
are going to get cancer,

21:23.867 --> 21:25.618
and God knows how many more will die.

21:26.161 --> 21:27.203
Yes, very good.

21:27.996 --> 21:30.123
There has been
an accident at Chernobyl,

21:30.206 --> 21:32.000
but I've been assured
there is no problem.

21:32.333 --> 21:33.835
I'm telling you that there is.

21:34.377 --> 21:36.004
I prefer my opinion to yours.

21:36.796 --> 21:38.590
I'm a nuclear physicist.

21:39.841 --> 21:42.844
Before you were Deputy Secretary,
you worked in a shoe factory.

21:44.345 --> 21:46.973
Yes, I worked in a shoe factory.

21:48.808 --> 21:50.059
And now, I'm in charge.

21:53.188 --> 21:54.689
"To the workers of the world."

22:17.712 --> 22:22.425
Stable iodine will keep your thyroid
from absorbing radioactive iodine.

22:23.927 --> 22:26.012
Take one a day
for as long as they last.

22:26.804 --> 22:27.931
And go east.

22:28.890 --> 22:30.517
Get as far from Minsk as you can.

23:04.801 --> 23:05.802
It's overkill.

23:06.344 --> 23:08.471
Pikalov's showing off
to make us look bad.

23:08.555 --> 23:10.598
It doesn't matter how it looks.

23:11.391 --> 23:13.184
Shcherbina is a pure bureaucrat,

23:13.768 --> 23:15.770
as stupid as he is pigheaded.

23:16.312 --> 23:18.273
We'll tell him the truth
in the simplest terms possible.

23:18.356 --> 23:19.566
We'll be fine.

23:23.111 --> 23:24.112
Pikalov!

23:50.513 --> 23:53.182
Comrade Shcherbina,
Chief Engineer Fomin,

23:53.266 --> 23:56.477
Colonel General Pikalov and I
are honored at your arrival.

23:56.561 --> 23:57.895
Deeply, deeply honored.

23:58.187 --> 24:01.149
Naturally, we regret
the circumstances of your visit,

24:01.232 --> 24:03.276
but, as you can see,
we are making excellent progress

24:03.359 --> 24:04.944
in containing the damage.

24:05.028 --> 24:10.033
We have begun our own inquiry
into the cause of the accident,

24:10.116 --> 24:13.161
and I have a list of individuals

24:13.244 --> 24:15.496
who we believe are accountable.

24:38.394 --> 24:39.812
Professor Legasov, I understand

24:39.896 --> 24:42.190
you've been saying dangerous things.

24:42.273 --> 24:43.650
Very dangerous things.

24:44.025 --> 24:45.943
Apparently, our reactor core exploded.

24:47.028 --> 24:50.907
Please, tell me how
an RBMK reactor core explodes.

24:53.284 --> 24:55.328
I'm not prepared to explain it
at this time.

24:56.079 --> 24:58.456
As I presumed, he has no answer.

24:58.539 --> 25:00.833
It's disgraceful, really.

25:01.709 --> 25:04.295
To spread disinformation
at a time like this.

25:10.802 --> 25:13.137
Why did I see graphite on the roof?

25:14.097 --> 25:15.723
Graphite is only found in the core,

25:15.807 --> 25:18.726
where it's used as
a neutron flux moderator.

25:19.227 --> 25:20.269
Correct?

25:21.604 --> 25:25.149
Fomin, why did the Deputy Chairman
see graphite on the roof?

25:26.234 --> 25:27.610
Well, that can't be.

25:28.986 --> 25:31.656
Comrade Shcherbina,
my apologies, but graphite...

25:31.864 --> 25:32.907
That's not possible.

25:33.074 --> 25:35.159
Perhaps you saw burnt concrete.

25:35.243 --> 25:36.577
Now there you made a mistake,

25:37.203 --> 25:39.622
because I may not know much
about nuclear reactors,

25:40.081 --> 25:41.374
but I know a lot about concrete.

25:42.333 --> 25:44.711
- Comrade, I assure you...
- I understand.

25:45.586 --> 25:47.255
You think Legasov is wrong?

25:48.506 --> 25:49.716
How shall we prove it?

25:51.718 --> 25:53.886
Our high-range dosimeter just arrived.

25:55.012 --> 25:57.223
We could cover one of our trucks
with lead shielding,

25:58.057 --> 25:59.684
mount the dosimeter on the front.

26:06.232 --> 26:08.901
Have one of your men
get as close to the fire as he can.

26:09.569 --> 26:11.237
Give him every bit
of protection you have.

26:11.320 --> 26:14.824
But understand that even with
lead shielding, it may not be enough.

26:17.326 --> 26:18.494
Then I'll do it myself.

26:23.958 --> 26:24.959
Good.

28:23.703 --> 28:24.745
He's back.

28:54.483 --> 28:55.651
It's not three roentgen.

28:56.485 --> 28:57.486
It's 15,000.

28:58.863 --> 29:00.156
Comrade Shcherbina...

29:01.324 --> 29:02.825
What does that number mean?

29:02.909 --> 29:04.827
It means the core is open.

29:05.536 --> 29:07.663
It means the fire
we're watching with our own eyes

29:07.747 --> 29:09.290
is giving off nearly
twice the radiation

29:09.373 --> 29:11.125
released by the bomb in Hiroshima.

29:11.208 --> 29:13.044
And that's every single hour.

29:13.127 --> 29:16.088
Hour after hour,
20 hours since the explosion,

29:16.172 --> 29:17.798
so 40 bombs' worth by now.

29:18.049 --> 29:19.216
Forty-eight more tomorrow.

29:19.508 --> 29:22.053
And it will not stop.
Not in a week, not in a month.

29:22.136 --> 29:23.679
It will burn and spread its poison

29:23.763 --> 29:25.848
until the entire continent is dead.

29:29.685 --> 29:31.896
Please escort Comrades
Bryukhanov and Fomin

29:31.979 --> 29:33.522
to the local party headquarters.

29:34.357 --> 29:35.816
- Thank you for your service.
- Comrade...

29:35.900 --> 29:37.068
You're excused.

29:37.944 --> 29:39.570
Dyatlov was in charge.

29:41.197 --> 29:42.490
It was Dyatlov!

29:42.949 --> 29:45.534
- Tell me how to put it out.
- We'll use helicopters.

29:45.785 --> 29:47.370
We'll drop water on it
like a forest fire.

29:47.453 --> 29:50.164
No, no, no. You don't understand.
This isn't a fire.

29:51.082 --> 29:55.002
This is a fissioning reactor core
burning at over 2,000 degrees.

29:55.086 --> 29:56.796
The heat will instantly
vaporize the water...

29:56.879 --> 29:58.089
How do we put it out?

30:01.342 --> 30:03.344
You are dealing with something

30:03.427 --> 30:07.348
that has never occurred
on this planet before.

30:09.850 --> 30:10.851
Boron.

30:12.395 --> 30:13.854
Boron and sand.

30:14.772 --> 30:17.316
Well, that'll create problems
of its own,

30:17.400 --> 30:19.110
but I don't see any other way.

30:19.860 --> 30:21.654
Of course, it's going to take
thousands of drops,

30:21.737 --> 30:24.323
because you can't fly the helicopters
directly over the core,

30:24.407 --> 30:25.992
so most of it is going to miss.

30:26.075 --> 30:28.244
- How much sand and boron?
- Well, I can't be...

30:28.327 --> 30:31.664
- For God's sake, roughly!
- Five thousand tons.

30:32.456 --> 30:35.209
And obviously, we're going to need
to evacuate an enormous area...

30:35.292 --> 30:36.919
Never mind that. Focus on the fire.

30:37.003 --> 30:39.046
I am focusing on the fire.

30:39.672 --> 30:43.050
The wind,
it's carrying all that smoke.

30:44.093 --> 30:45.636
All that radiation.

30:46.053 --> 30:48.889
At least evacuate Pripyat.
It's three kilometers away.

30:48.973 --> 30:51.017
- That's my decision to make.
- Then make it.

30:51.100 --> 30:53.811
- I've been told not to.
- Is it or is not your decision?

30:53.894 --> 30:55.062
I'm in charge here!

30:55.688 --> 30:57.314
This will go much easier
if you talk to me

30:57.398 --> 30:58.691
about the things you do understand

30:58.774 --> 31:00.860
and not about the things
you do not understand.

31:05.364 --> 31:06.657
Where are you going?

31:06.741 --> 31:09.952
I'm going to get you
5,000 tons of sand and boron.

31:18.419 --> 31:19.670
There's a hotel.

32:16.393 --> 32:17.645
I'd prefer one of those.

32:26.445 --> 32:28.030
Superstitious?

32:37.123 --> 32:38.833
Moscow?

32:42.336 --> 32:44.213
Are you here because of the fire?

32:49.093 --> 32:51.512
Anything we should be worried about?

32:56.767 --> 32:57.810
No.

34:03.334 --> 34:05.920
We're in position.
Approaching drop.

34:07.254 --> 34:08.589
Clear to proceed.

34:10.549 --> 34:13.761
Copy.
We'll go one by one in rotation.

34:18.933 --> 34:20.726
Remind them about the perimeter.

34:24.480 --> 34:26.607
They cannot fly
directly over the fire.

34:26.690 --> 34:29.109
A minimum of a ten-meter perimeter.

34:29.193 --> 34:30.402
Ten-meter perimeter. Copy.

34:30.736 --> 34:34.281
Lead One, per pre-flight,
maintain minimum ten-meter perimeter.

34:34.823 --> 34:35.866
Copy that.

34:35.950 --> 34:39.453
Forty meters, 35, 30...

34:43.457 --> 34:44.458
No, no, no.

34:44.541 --> 34:46.293
They're too close.

34:46.377 --> 34:48.128
If they can't get over the fire,
how are they supposed...

34:48.212 --> 34:49.755
The wind will have to carry it.

34:49.838 --> 34:52.466
Tell them,
they cannot go over the core.

34:55.094 --> 34:56.303
Lead One is too close.

34:56.387 --> 34:58.806
I repeat, they are too close.

34:58.889 --> 35:00.140
Copy.

35:00.975 --> 35:03.102
Lead One,
you are inside the perimeter.

35:03.936 --> 35:07.189
Did not copy.
Signal's breaking up.

35:08.983 --> 35:10.025
Lead One?

35:11.485 --> 35:12.486
Do you copy?

35:13.445 --> 35:14.822
Lead One, do you copy?

35:15.823 --> 35:17.700
Lead One, come in.

35:17.783 --> 35:19.535
Do you copy, Lead One?

35:19.618 --> 35:20.786
Lead One!

35:39.346 --> 35:41.473
Sir?
What do I tell the others?

35:44.184 --> 35:46.103
Is there any other way, Legasov?

35:54.695 --> 35:55.863
Send the next one in.

35:58.073 --> 35:59.658
Tell them to approach from the west.

36:00.826 --> 36:03.912
Lead Two,
approach from the west.

36:25.225 --> 36:27.186
Kurchatov Institute Laboratory Four.

36:27.936 --> 36:31.648
Marina Gruzinskaya,
it's Ulana Khomyuk from Minsk.

36:34.193 --> 36:35.194
Oh, yes.

36:35.277 --> 36:37.821
How nice of you to phone.
It's been too long.

36:37.905 --> 36:39.031
It has.

36:39.114 --> 36:41.742
I was actually calling
about our friend.

36:41.825 --> 36:44.078
- You know, the one in the country.
- Oh, yes, of course.

36:44.161 --> 36:47.706
I wanted to see how he's doing.
It's so hot there right now.

36:47.790 --> 36:50.250
Yes, it's extremely hot,

36:50.334 --> 36:51.835
but his nephews are flying down,

36:51.919 --> 36:53.837
and they always bring cool weather.

36:53.921 --> 36:55.339
Oh, which nephews?

36:56.507 --> 36:59.760
Simka, who's 14,
and little Boris, who's five.

37:00.302 --> 37:01.678
Well, that's wonderful.

37:01.762 --> 37:03.639
Though children can
make you even hotter

37:03.722 --> 37:05.307
when they're crawling all over you.

37:05.391 --> 37:06.642
Maybe I should visit them.

37:06.725 --> 37:08.852
No, they don't want visitors.

37:08.936 --> 37:11.021
I'm sorry, um,
I have to get back to work.

37:11.105 --> 37:13.023
It's very busy right now. Goodbye.

37:16.402 --> 37:18.737
They're dropping sand
and boron on the fire.

37:19.947 --> 37:21.031
It's what I would do.

37:23.158 --> 37:24.993
Yes, I'm sure it is.

37:47.349 --> 37:48.559
Where are you going?

37:49.643 --> 37:50.644
Chernobyl.

37:58.986 --> 38:01.822
It's been smooth. Twenty drops.

38:07.494 --> 38:09.079
What?

38:11.665 --> 38:15.335
There are 50,000 people
in this city.

38:16.628 --> 38:19.923
Professor Ilyin,
who's also on the commission,

38:20.007 --> 38:22.843
says the radiation isn't
high enough to evacuate.

38:22.926 --> 38:25.095
Ilyin isn't a physicist.

38:25.179 --> 38:27.681
Well, he's a medical doctor.
If he says it's safe, it's safe.

38:27.764 --> 38:30.267
- Not if they stay here.
- We're staying here.

38:30.350 --> 38:33.687
Yes, we are, and we'll be dead
in five years.

38:42.529 --> 38:44.907
I'm sorry, I...

38:44.990 --> 38:46.575
I'm sorry.

39:25.030 --> 39:27.115
Shcherbina.

39:37.543 --> 39:38.794
Thank you.

39:45.467 --> 39:49.263
A nuclear plant in Sweden
has detected radiation

39:49.346 --> 39:52.391
and identified it
as a by-product of our fuel.

39:53.517 --> 39:55.894
The Americans took satellite photos.

39:57.271 --> 40:00.440
The reactor building,
the smoke, the fire.

40:02.985 --> 40:04.695
The whole world knows.

40:11.702 --> 40:13.787
The wind has been blowing
toward Germany.

40:15.747 --> 40:18.083
They're not letting
children play outside

40:20.127 --> 40:21.587
in Frankfurt.

40:33.348 --> 40:34.391
Want a smoke?

40:50.782 --> 40:53.327
There has been a nuclear
accident in the Soviet Union

40:53.410 --> 40:55.203
and the Soviets
have admitted that it happened.

40:55.287 --> 40:56.663
The Soviet version is this,

40:56.788 --> 40:58.290
One of the atomic reactors

40:58.373 --> 41:01.627
at the Chernobyl Atomic Power Plant
near the city of Kiev was damaged,

41:01.710 --> 41:03.545
and there is speculation in Moscow

41:03.629 --> 41:06.048
that people were injured
and may have died.

41:06.131 --> 41:09.051
The Soviets may have been fairly quick
to acknowledge the accident

41:09.134 --> 41:12.471
because evidence in the form
of mild nuclear radiation

41:12.554 --> 41:16.016
had already reached beyond
the Soviet borders to Scandinavia.

45:31.187 --> 45:34.107
I said turn around.
This is a restricted zone.

45:34.190 --> 45:36.484
I'm from the Byelorussian
Institute for Nuclear Energy.

45:36.568 --> 45:38.111
Do you have permission?

45:38.194 --> 45:40.697
Listen to me, I need to speak
to someone urgently.

45:40.780 --> 45:41.990
Turn around right now,

45:42.073 --> 45:43.116
or I'll arrest you.

45:43.199 --> 45:47.037
If you arrest me, you should take me
to the highest possible authority.

45:51.625 --> 45:54.169
We have to start
a radiological survey.

45:54.252 --> 45:57.923
Sector by sector, on foot,
dosimeters in hand.

45:59.883 --> 46:01.259
Are you all right?

46:04.054 --> 46:05.388
Of course.

46:06.848 --> 46:11.019
Comrades... The guards arrested
this woman at the south checkpoint.

46:11.102 --> 46:12.687
I'd have put her in a cell...

46:12.771 --> 46:15.106
But he thought you should
know that I know.

46:15.190 --> 46:17.150
I know that
your reactor core is exposed.

46:17.233 --> 46:19.986
I know the graphite's on fire,
the fuel is melting,

46:20.070 --> 46:22.197
and you're dropping
sand and boron on it,

46:22.280 --> 46:25.200
which you probably thought was
smart, but you've made a mistake.

46:25.283 --> 46:28.620
Ulana Yuriyvna Khomyuk,
Chief Physicist,

46:28.703 --> 46:30.914
Byelorussian Institute
for Nuclear Energy.

46:30.997 --> 46:34.417
And you are
Valery Alexeyevich Legasov?

46:34.501 --> 46:36.836
Smothering the core
will put the fire out,

46:36.920 --> 46:39.798
but the temperature will
eventually increase...

46:39.881 --> 46:41.424
Believe me,
I'm perfectly aware.

46:41.508 --> 46:43.551
But I estimate at least a month

46:43.635 --> 46:45.470
before it melts through
the lower concrete pad,

46:45.553 --> 46:46.680
- which gives us time...
- No.

46:46.763 --> 46:49.140
You don't have a month.
You have approximately two days.

46:50.266 --> 46:51.434
Yes,

46:51.518 --> 46:53.520
the fuel would take a month

46:53.603 --> 46:55.605
to reach the concrete pad here.

46:56.064 --> 46:57.065
But first...

46:58.483 --> 47:01.695
it's going to burn through
the biological shield here by Tuesday.

47:01.778 --> 47:03.238
And when it does,

47:03.321 --> 47:06.241
it's going to hit these tanks,
bubbler pools, reservoirs...

47:06.324 --> 47:09.202
Reservoirs for the ECS.
I understand your concern,

47:09.285 --> 47:11.413
but I confirmed it
with plant personnel.

47:11.496 --> 47:13.790
- The tanks are nearly empty.
- No, they were nearly empty.

47:15.041 --> 47:17.877
Each of these points here, here,

47:17.961 --> 47:20.588
and here all drain
to the bubbler pools.

47:20.672 --> 47:23.341
I'm guessing that every pipe
in the building ruptured.

47:23.425 --> 47:26.469
And then there are those fire engines
that I saw on the way in.

47:26.553 --> 47:28.638
The fire hoses are still connected.

47:29.389 --> 47:32.100
They've been gushing water
into the structure this whole time.

47:32.183 --> 47:33.476
The tanks are full.

47:34.978 --> 47:37.313
The tanks are full.

47:40.775 --> 47:43.486
From the Council
of Ministers of the USSR.

47:43.820 --> 47:48.283
An accident has occurred
at Chernobyl Nuclear Power Station.

47:48.366 --> 47:51.578
One of the nuclear reactors
has been damaged.

47:51.661 --> 47:55.331
Measures are being taken to address
the consequences of the accident.

47:55.415 --> 47:58.043
Victims are receiving assistance.

47:58.126 --> 48:00.795
A government commission
has been formed.

48:17.187 --> 48:21.399
I have 10 minutes,
then I'm back on the phone

48:21.483 --> 48:24.402
apologizing to our friends,

48:24.486 --> 48:26.863
apologizing to our enemies.

48:27.947 --> 48:31.201
Our power comes
from the perception of our power.

48:31.868 --> 48:34.913
Do you understand
the damage this has done?

48:34.996 --> 48:37.040
Do you understand what's at stake?

48:39.375 --> 48:40.627
Boris.

48:43.171 --> 48:47.092
Professor Legasov
will deliver our briefing.

48:49.302 --> 48:50.845
There is some good news.

48:50.929 --> 48:53.640
The air drops are working
to douse the fire.

48:53.723 --> 48:57.519
There's been a reduction
in radionuclide emissions,

48:57.602 --> 49:01.689
but the fire will not be extinguished
for at least another two weeks.

49:04.234 --> 49:07.570
There is also an additional problem.

49:08.655 --> 49:10.490
Nuclear fuel doesn't turn cold

49:10.573 --> 49:13.284
simply because it is not on fire.

49:13.368 --> 49:14.661
In fact, the temperature
will likely rise

49:14.744 --> 49:17.664
as a result of the blanket of sand
we've dropped.

49:17.747 --> 49:20.583
The uranium will melt the sand,

49:20.667 --> 49:22.544
creating a kind of lava

49:22.627 --> 49:25.755
which will begin to melt down
through the shield below.

49:25.839 --> 49:27.507
You have made lava?

49:29.717 --> 49:31.678
I anticipated this.

49:33.721 --> 49:38.143
I believed there was time to reinforce
this lower concrete pad

49:38.226 --> 49:41.354
before the lava reached the Earth
and contaminated the groundwater.

49:41.437 --> 49:44.566
But as it turned out, I was
worried about the wrong thing.

49:44.649 --> 49:46.359
Uh...

49:46.442 --> 49:49.988
It was my understanding
that these large water tanks

49:50.071 --> 49:52.782
under the reactor
were essentially empty.

49:52.866 --> 49:55.952
This is Ulana Khomyuk
of the Byelorussian Institute.

49:56.035 --> 49:58.204
Thanks to her insight,
we are now aware

49:58.288 --> 50:01.749
that the tanks are, in fact, full.

50:01.833 --> 50:05.378
Of water.
Why is that a problem, Professor?

50:09.716 --> 50:11.509
When the lava enters these tanks,

50:11.593 --> 50:14.012
it will instantly
superheat and vaporize

50:14.095 --> 50:17.765
approximately 7,000
cubic meters of water,

50:17.849 --> 50:21.227
causing a significant
thermal explosion.

50:21.936 --> 50:23.563
How significant?

50:23.646 --> 50:27.400
We estimate
between two and four megatons.

50:27.859 --> 50:30.445
Everything within
a 30-kilometer radius

50:30.528 --> 50:32.447
will be completely destroyed,

50:32.530 --> 50:35.909
including the three remaining
reactors at Chernobyl.

50:35.992 --> 50:39.370
The entirety of the radioactive
material in all of the cores

50:39.454 --> 50:42.665
will be ejected at force

50:42.749 --> 50:44.792
and dispersed by a massive shock wave,

50:46.002 --> 50:48.129
which will extend

50:48.213 --> 50:51.049
approximately 200 kilometers

50:51.132 --> 50:54.177
and likely be fatal
to the entire population of Kiev

50:54.260 --> 50:56.054
as well as a portion of Minsk.

50:56.638 --> 50:59.474
The release of radiation
will be severe

50:59.557 --> 51:01.100
and will impact

51:01.184 --> 51:05.438
all of Soviet Ukraine,
Latvia, Lithuania,

51:05.521 --> 51:09.943
Byelorussia, as well as Poland,
Czechoslovakia, Hungary,

51:10.026 --> 51:12.320
Romania, and most of East Germany.

51:16.199 --> 51:18.993
What do you mean "impact?"

51:19.994 --> 51:22.580
For much of the area,
a nearly permanent disruption

51:22.664 --> 51:24.374
of the food and water supply,

51:25.458 --> 51:28.294
a steep increase in the rates
of cancer and birth defects.

51:28.378 --> 51:30.880
I don't know how many deaths
there will be, but many.

51:31.798 --> 51:33.633
For Byelorussia and the Ukraine,

51:33.716 --> 51:36.386
"impact" means
completely uninhabitable

51:36.469 --> 51:38.972
for a minimum of 100 years.

51:43.476 --> 51:46.813
There are more than 50 million people

51:46.896 --> 51:49.607
living in Byelorussia and Ukraine.

51:49.691 --> 51:51.651
Sixty, yes.

51:55.989 --> 51:57.740
And how long before this happens?

51:58.241 --> 52:00.618
Approximately 48 to 72 hours.

52:05.373 --> 52:07.625
But we may have a solution.

52:07.709 --> 52:09.669
We can pump the water from the tanks.

52:09.752 --> 52:12.922
Unfortunately, the tanks are
sealed shut by a sluice gate,

52:13.006 --> 52:14.632
and the gate can only be opened

52:14.716 --> 52:16.676
manually from within
the duct system itself.

52:16.759 --> 52:20.054
So, we need to find
three plant workers

52:20.138 --> 52:22.557
who know the facility well enough
to enter the basement,

52:22.640 --> 52:27.478
here, find their way through
all these duct ways,

52:27.562 --> 52:29.939
get to the sluice gate valve here,

52:30.023 --> 52:34.277
and give us the access we need
to pump out the tanks.

52:34.610 --> 52:37.238
Of course, we will need
your permission.

52:37.530 --> 52:39.365
My permission for what?

52:39.449 --> 52:42.035
Uh, the water in these ducts,

52:42.118 --> 52:44.912
the level of
radioactive contamination...

52:44.996 --> 52:46.789
They'll likely be dead in a week.

52:49.125 --> 52:52.670
We're asking for your permission
to kill three men.

53:00.803 --> 53:02.472
Well...

53:02.555 --> 53:05.725
Comrade Legasov,

53:05.808 --> 53:08.478
all victories inevitably
come at a cost.

54:34.105 --> 54:37.150
And open the sluice gate valve, here.

54:37.942 --> 54:41.946
The valve will be difficult
to operate, so we'll need three men

54:42.029 --> 54:45.616
who will need to know
the basement layout.

54:45.700 --> 54:49.495
And, of course, any volunteers
will be rewarded.

54:49.996 --> 54:53.332
A yearly stipend of 400 rubles.

55:00.298 --> 55:05.052
And, uh, for those of you working
in reactors one and two, promotions.

55:05.136 --> 55:08.931
Why are reactors
one and two still operating at all?

55:09.474 --> 55:12.768
My friend was a security guard
that night, and, uh, she's now dying.

55:13.352 --> 55:15.605
And we've all heard about the firemen.

55:15.688 --> 55:19.650
And now you want us to swim
underneath a burning reactor?

55:20.318 --> 55:22.361
Do you even know
how contaminated it is?

55:24.697 --> 55:26.532
I don't have an exact number.

55:26.616 --> 55:29.827
You don't need an exact number
to know if it will kill us.

55:29.911 --> 55:31.162
But you can't even tell us that.

55:33.289 --> 55:35.625
Why should we do this?
For what, 400 rubles?

55:39.253 --> 55:41.380
You'll do it because it must be done.

55:46.552 --> 55:49.096
You'll do it because nobody else can.

55:52.225 --> 55:55.811
And if you don't, millions will die.

55:57.063 --> 55:59.398
If you tell me that's not enough,
I won't believe you.

56:01.234 --> 56:05.363
This is what has always
set our people apart.

56:05.446 --> 56:08.366
A thousand years of sacrifice
in our veins.

56:09.408 --> 56:11.869
And every generation
must know its own suffering.

56:12.912 --> 56:15.998
I spit on the people who did this,

56:16.082 --> 56:18.000
and I curse the price I have to pay.

56:21.128 --> 56:25.091
But I'm making my peace with it,
now you make yours.

56:25.633 --> 56:27.301
And go into that water.

56:29.845 --> 56:31.681
Because it must be done.

56:48.823 --> 56:50.283
Ananenko.

56:54.620 --> 56:56.080
Bezpalov.

57:01.043 --> 57:02.712
Baranov.
